---
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CodeCard from "../components/CodeCard.astro";
import { GITHUB_NATS_EXAMPLES_URL } from "../lib/site";

const quickStart = `# once: generate a self-signed TLS cert into tls/
./scripts/gen-dev-cert.sh

# bring up NATS + quik (--features nats) + two backends w/ sidecars
docker compose -f examples/nats/homelab/docker-compose.yml up -d --build`;

const operatorLoop = `# what's registered - NATS' view
nats kv ls    quik_registrations
nats kv watch quik_registrations 'reg.shop.checkout.>'   # live add/delete feed

# what quik resolved it into
curl -s localhost:9090/admin/pools/checkout | jq '.members[].address'

# operator-drain an instance - durable override, applied by the watcher
nats kv put quik_registrations override.shop.checkout.checkout-1 '{"action":"drain"}'
nats kv del quik_registrations override.shop.checkout.checkout-1   # undrain

# kill a backend → it stops heartbeating → TTL expires → drained (~30s)
docker compose -f examples/nats/homelab/docker-compose.yml stop checkout-1

# observe
curl -s localhost:9090/metrics | grep -E 'quik_nats_|quik_pool_member_'`;

const allowList = `[[upstreams]]
name = "checkout"

[upstreams.nats]
# The KV subtree this pool registers from (required for a NATS-backed pool).
subject         = "reg.shop.checkout.>"
# Gate which addresses a self-asserted registration may claim. A registration
# outside this list is rejected (quik_nats_registration_rejected_total).
allow_addresses = ["192.168.0.0/16"]   # in the demos: [".svc"] (a docker alias)`;

const stacks = [
  {
    icon: "lucide:house",
    name: "homelab",
    topology: "1 NATS node · 1 quik · 2 backends",
    use: "Single host or homelab. Comes up in one command.",
  },
  {
    icon: "lucide:server",
    name: "ha",
    topology: "3-node NATS cluster (R3 bucket) · 2 quik",
    use: "Production-shaped: replicated bucket, multiple proxies converging independently.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Deploy a NATS registration stack - quik.sh"
  description="Run quik with NATS service registration: the homelab and HA example stacks, the operator loop, address allow-lists, and securing NATS with decentralised JWT credentials."
>
  <article class="py-12 md:py-16">
    <a
      href="/service-registration"
      class="inline-flex items-center text-sm text-slate-400 hover:text-white"
    >
      <Icon name="lucide:arrow-left" class="mr-2 h-4 w-4" />
      Service registration
    </a>

    <header class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.24em] text-cyan-300">
        Deployment
      </p>
      <h1
        class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.045em] md:text-6xl"
      >
        Deploy a NATS registration stack.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
        Two runnable stacks build quik with <code
          class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-slate-200"
          >--features nats</code
        > and run a quik-register sidecar beside each backend. Start with the
        homelab stack; the HA stack is the same shape with a replicated bucket
        and a NATS cluster.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
        stacks.map((s) => (
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.03] p-6 backdrop-blur-xl">
            <Icon name={s.icon} class="mb-4 h-6 w-6 text-cyan-300" />
            <h2 class="font-mono text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-cyan-100">
              {s.name}/
            </h2>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-300">{s.topology}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-400">{s.use}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
        1. Bring up the homelab stack
      </h2>
      <CodeCard caption="terminal" code={quickStart} class="mt-6" />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
        2. The operator loop
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-slate-400">
        Inspect what's registered, watch quik resolve it, drain an instance with
        a durable override, and watch a dead backend fall out as its lease
        expires.
      </p>
      <CodeCard caption="terminal" code={operatorLoop} class="mt-6" />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">
        3. Constrain what can register
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-slate-400">
        Registration is self-asserted, so gate the addresses a pool will accept
        with <code
          class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-slate-200"
          >allow_addresses</code
        >. Keep it as tight as the deployment allows - typically your LAN CIDR.
      </p>
      <CodeCard caption="quik.toml" code={allowList} class="mt-6" />
    </section>

    <section
      class="mt-14 rounded-[1.5rem] border border-amber-300/20 bg-amber-300/[0.05] p-6 backdrop-blur-xl md:p-8"
    >
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <Icon
          name="lucide:shield-alert"
          class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-amber-300"
        />
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">
            Secure NATS before you leave the LAN
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-slate-400">
            The bundled stacks run no-auth NATS so they come up in one command -
            fine on a laptop or trusted LAN, never beyond it. For anything real,
            use decentralised JWT credentials: services get a key scoped to
            publish <code
              class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.85em] text-slate-200"
              >reg.&lt;namespace&gt;.*</code
            > only, operators a separate key for the override namespace.
          </p>
          <CodeCard
            caption="terminal"
            code={`# nsc: operator + account + scoped .creds files
./examples/nats/nats/bootstrap-creds.sh`}
            class="mt-4 border-amber-300/10"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p class="mt-12 max-w-3xl leading-8 text-slate-400">
      Both stacks, the credential bootstrap, and the full key namespace and
      permissions model live in <a
        href={GITHUB_NATS_EXAMPLES_URL}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
        class="text-cyan-200 underline-offset-4 hover:underline">examples/nats</a
      > and the service-registration reference in the repo docs.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
